title: Systemd Timesyncd
agents: linux
catalog: os/services
license: GPLv2
distribution: check_mk
description:
 This check uses the output of {timedatectl timesync-status} as sent by the
 agent. It lists the information of the synchronised time server including
 the server address, stratum, offset and jitter. The check gets critical
 if the time offset exceeds a default level, if the stratum is too high or
 other error conditions. The stratum levels default to 10 (warning) / 16
 (critical). Critical is set to 16 because that is the special NTP value for
 an unsynchronised clock and thus an actual error, whereas any stratum below
 it (1 to 15) is a valid, synchronised state. The warning at 10 flags an
 unusually deep chain to the reference clock: such hops are not an error in
 themselves, but each one adds latency and reduces accuracy, so a chain that
 long is bad practice and worth a heads-up. The jitter (the short-term
 variation of the time synchronisation) is reported as a metric but has no
 thresholds by default, as it is a stability signal rather than a health
 metric. Optional jitter levels, independent of the time offset levels, can
 be configured via the ruleset. It gets UNKNOWN if the time has not yet been
 synchronised. The check logic is shared with the ntp check. On a running
 instance the time is synchronised either using ntp or timesyncd. The agent
 therefore determines the correct service to use and subsequent check.

 Note: this check is only available for versions higher than Ubuntu 18.04

discovery:
 One service is created if the {timedatectl timesync-status} command
 is available.
